[2026-10-13T14:53:54.820Z] [TOOL_CALL] {"id":"call_00_w6a9b0nJQPnQ8tFY8KRL7189","name":"apply_patch","args":{"patch":"--- a/kiwi/openqos/disk.xml\n+++ b/kiwi/openqos/disk.xml\n@@ -11,13 +11,27 @@\n   them lets every profile share the same base packages and account setup while only\n   the disk-based ones inherit this type.\n \n-  The type is modelled on Fedora's `Cloud-Base-Generic` aarch64 type in\n-  upstream/teams/cloud/cloud.xml, which is the closest *proven* configuration for\n-  an aarch64 system that boots under UEFI in a virtual machine. Deriving from\n-  something Fedora actually ships is deliberate: aarch64 boot layout (ESP size,\n-  btrfs subvolumes, GRUB on a serial console) is easy to get subtly wrong from\n-  first principles.\n+  Both types are modelled on Fedora's `Cloud-Base-Generic` types in\n+  upstream/teams/cloud/cloud.xml, which are the closest *proven* configuration for\n+  a system that boots under UEFI in a virtual machine. Fedora defines one per\n+  architecture and so do we. Deriving from something Fedora actually ships is\n+  deliberate: the boot layout (ESP size, btrfs subvolumes, GRUB on a serial\n+  console) is easy to get subtly wrong from first principles.\n+\n+  The two blocks differ in exactly one thing: the kernel's serial console. aarch64's\n+  PL011 is `ttyAMA0`; the PC's 16550 is `ttyS0`. Fedora keeps the filesystem, the\n+  firmware, the ESP size and the GRUB console identical across the two, and so do\n+  we. They cannot be merged into one <type>: `arch` is a preference attribute, so a\n+  single block cannot carry a different command line per architecture.\n \n   Deviations from that upstream type, and why:\n@@ -32,7 +46,7 @@\n <image>\n \t<profiles>\n-\t\t<profile name=\"OpenQOS-Disk\" description=\"Shared OpenQOS disk-image layout (aarch64 UEFI)\">\n+\t\t<profile name=\"OpenQOS-Disk\" description=\"Shared OpenQOS disk-image layout (aarch64 and x86_64, UEFI)\">\n \t\t\t<requires profile=\"OpenQOS-Base\"/>\n@@ -69,4 +83,30 @@\n \t\t\t</oemconfig>\n \t\t</type>\n \t</preferences>\n+\n+\t<!--\n+\t  The x86_64 sibling of the block above. Same layout, same firmware, same GRUB\n+\t  console; the serial console moves from the PL011 to the 16550. Kept as a\n+\t  separate <type> because the kernel command line is architecture-specific and\n+\t  `arch` is how KIWI selects a preference block.\n+\t-->\n+\t<preferences profiles=\"OpenQOS-Disk\" arch=\"x86_64\">\n+\t\t<type image=\"oem\" format=\"qcow2\"\n+\t\t      filesystem=\"btrfs\" btrfs_root_is_subvolume=\"true\" btrfs_set_default_volume=\"false\" fsmountoptions=\"compress=zstd:1\"\n+\t\t      kernelcmdline=\"no_timer_check console=tty1 console=ttyS0,115200n8 systemd.firstboot=off\"\n+\t\t      devicepersistency=\"by-uuid\"\n+\t\t      bootpartition=\"false\" efipartsize=\"100\" firmware=\"uefi\"\n+\t\t      rootfs_label=\"openqos\"\n+\t\t      >\n+\t\t\t<bootloader name=\"grub2\" bls=\"true\" console=\"serial\" timeout=\"5\"/>\n+\t\t\t<size unit=\"G\">16</size>\n+\t\t\t<systemdisk>\n+\t\t\t\t<volume name=\"@root=root\"/>\n+\t\t\t\t<volume name=\"boot\" parent=\"/\"/>\n+\t\t\t\t<volume name=\"home\" parent=\"/\"/>\n+\t\t\t\t<volume name=\"var\" parent=\"/\"/>\n+\t\t\t</systemdisk>\n+\t\t\t<oemconfig>\n+\t\t\t\t<oem-resize>false</oem-resize>\n+\t\t\t</oemconfig>\n+\t\t</type>\n+\t</preferences>\n </image>\n*** End Patch"}}
