[2026-10-13T15:05:14.346Z] [ASSISTANT] Waiting on the container commit to finish, then I'll swap in a container that bind-mounts the host's `/dev`. Let me explain the diagnosis while that runs.

KIWI's disk stage does three things in sequence: attach the raw image to a loop device, write a partition table with `parted`, then refer to the partitions as `/dev/loop33p1`, `/dev/loop33p2`. Those partition nodes don't exist as static files — the kernel announces them via a uevent and **udev** creates the node. This container has no udev running and its `/dev` is a private tmpfs, so the nodes never appear even though the kernel knows the partitions exist.

Creating the nodes by hand can't work either, because the partition count and offsets aren't known until `parted` has run. Bind-mounting the host's `/dev` sidesteps this entirely: this Ubuntu host runs `systemd-udevd`, so when the container attaches the loop device the host's udev creates `/dev/loop33p1` in the shared `/dev`, and KIWI sees it immediately. That's why I committed the container first — the new container keeps the toolchain and the cached RPMs, and only the `/dev` wiring changes.
