[2026-10-13T16:06:49.627Z] [ASSISTANT] Done. An x86_64 OpenQOS builds, boots, and reaches a graphical desktop on this machine.

## What this machine actually is

`x86_64` — Intel Xeon X5675 (Westmere, 12 threads), which matches the target architecture natively. But the host OS is **Ubuntu 24.04**, and OpenQOS's entire build path is Fedora-only tooling. The repo's docs assume a macOS host driving a Lima Fedora VM; neither applies here. `kiwi-ng`, `dnf5`, `rpmbuild` and `createrepo_c` are all absent.

So I built inside a **privileged `fedora:44` container**, which gives a native x86_64 Fedora build host — functionally what `tools/lima/openqos-dev.yaml` provisions.

## Code changes (2 files, uncommitted)

**`scripts/build-image.sh`** — it had a blanket guard rejecting any host that wasn't `aarch64`:

```bash
if [[ "${host_arch}" != "aarch64" ]]; then
    echo "error: this description defines only an aarch64 image type," >&2
    ...
    exit 1
fi
```

Replaced with an allowlist for `aarch64` and `x86_64`, rejecting others with an error that names the missing architecture rather than letting KIWI fail later with a bare "no image type defined". `bash -n` passes.

**`kiwi/openqos/disk.xml`** — added an `arch="x86_64"` image type next to the aarch64 one, and corrected the header comment. I derived it from Fedora's own `Cloud-Base-Generic` x86_64 preferences in `kiwi/upstream/teams/cloud/cloud.xml`, which is what the existing aarch64 block was modelled on. Fedora's x86_64 and aarch64 blocks differ in **exactly one attribute** — the kernel serial console, `ttyS0` vs `ttyAMA0` — so the new block is a faithful sibling, not a guess. `BootCore`/`BootCoreLive` are arch-neutral upstream, so the profile graph needed no changes.

`tests/check-upstream-drift.sh` still passes, so the upstream-delta guarantees are intact.

## Two container-level problems (not repo bugs)

Neither of these required a code change, and both are artefacts of building in Docker rather than the documented VM:

1. The Fedora image ships `/dev/loop-control` but no numbered `/dev/loopN` nodes, so KIWI's `losetup` failed with "failed to set up loop device".
2. Partition nodes `/dev/loopNpM` are created by **udev**, which isn't running in the container. Hand-creating them is impossible because the partition count isn't known until `parted` runs. Fixed by bind-mounting the host's `/dev`, so the host's udev populates the nodes the container sees.

## Artefacts built and verified

| Profile | Artefact | Size | Evidence |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `OpenQOS-Minimal` | `OpenQOS.x86_64-44.qcow2` | 868 MiB | Boots under UEFI to `openqos login:`, banner reads `OpenQOS Linux 0.1 (Development)` |
| `OpenQOS-KDE-Disk` | `OpenQOS.x86_64-44.qcow2` | 3.91 GiB | Boots to a **graphical session** |

Both live in `kiwi/build/<profile>/` with a `MANIFEST.txt`; both checksums verify. Contents were confirmed by mounting: subvolumes `root`/`boot`/`home`/`var`, `kernel-core 7.2.9-201.fc44.x86_64`, `openqos-release` + `openqos-branding`, `selinux-policy-targeted`, and `os-release` reporting `ID=openqos`. The KDE image carries `plasma-workspace`, `plasma-desktop`, `kwin`, `plasmalogin` and `plasma-setup` all at **6.7.5**, matching the README's verified baseline.

## One finding worth knowing

My first conclusion was wrong, and the repo had already documented why. The KDE image's serial log stops at a text login prompt and never mentions `graphical.target`, which looked like a broken desktop. It isn't: `tests/screenshot-image.sh` states that *"systemd stops logging there once the console moves, and a graphical greeter writes nothing to it at all."* I re-checked by framebuffer instead, using that script's technique — screendump via the QEMU monitor and judge by compressed pixel size. The screen jumped from ~10 KB (text console) to **841 KB** (wallpaper) at 51 s, so the desktop genuinely draws. Its systemd wiring is correct: `default.target` → `graphical.target`, which has `Wants=display-manager.service` → `plasmalogin.service`.

