## Where it is

```
/home/miroslav/Documents/workspace/OpenQOS/OpenQOS/kiwi/build/OpenQOS-KDE-Live/OpenQOS.x86_64-44.iso
```

Copy it out of the repo — `kiwi/build/` is gitignored, so it won't be committed.

| | |
| --- | --- |
| Path | `kiwi/build/OpenQOS-KDE-Live/OpenQOS.x86_64-44.iso` |
| Size | 3,517,693,952 bytes (3.28 GiB) |
| SHA-256 | `82da505ababefd4306c6fcb69cd2e8cfda2e0a29915e10682001cf9f07026641` |
| Volume label | `OpenQOS_KDE_Live` |
| Firmware | Hybrid — BIOS **and** UEFI |
| Kernel | 7.2.9-201.fc44.x86_64 |
| Desktop | KDE Plasma 6.7.5 |

`MANIFEST.txt` sits beside it with checksums for every artefact.

## Verified, not assumed

- Checksum matches the manifest.
- `file` reports ISO 9660 with a DOS/MBR boot sector, marked bootable.
- El Torito carries **two** boot images — a BIOS one (`/boot/x86_64/loader/eltorito.img`) and a UEFI one — so it will boot on either firmware.
- The kernel line is `root=live:CDLABEL=OpenQOS_KDE_Live` and the ISO's volume label is `OpenQOS_KDE_Live`. They agree, which matters: a mismatch there boots to a dracut prompt instead of a desktop.
- I booted it under UEFI in QEMU with the framebuffer check: it reached a drawn graphical session at 120 s (211 KB of compressed pixel content vs the 80 KB "graphical" threshold), sustained across three frames.

## Attaching it to your hypervisor

Attach it as a CD/DVD drive. Both UEFI and legacy BIOS work. Give it **at least 4 GiB RAM** and 2+ vCPUs; no persistent disk is needed for a live run.

One thing to expect: the GRUB default is the **second** entry, `Test this media & start OpenQOS`, which runs `rd.live.check` before booting. That comes from upstream's template, which sets `set default="1"`, and our derived copy deliberately changes only the menu class — so I did not alter it. If you'd rather go straight to the desktop, pick the first entry, `Start OpenQOS`.

## Code changes

For this ISO specifically:

- `kiwi/templates/grub-x86.cfg.iso-template` — **new**. Derived from Fedora's file at the pinned revision with one substitution (the GRUB menu class). Before this, an x86_64 ISO would have silently used upstream's template and shown Fedora's menu class.
- `tests/check-upstream-drift.sh` — the template check was hardcoded to the arm file. It now iterates a list, so both are verified. It passes: each body is identical to upstream except three `--class` lines.
- `kiwi/openqos/kde.xml` — the KDE profile descriptions said "aarch64" only.

Carried over from the earlier work: `scripts/build-image.sh` (arch allowlist) and `kiwi/openqos/disk.xml` (x86_64 image type).

## Caveats

The ISO is unsigned and the checksums are not GPG-signed — fine for your own testing, not for redistribution. I verified the live desktop boots; I did **not** exercise the installation path, and the roadmap still describes this as pre-alpha with no working installer.

If you want, I can also add `x86_64` support to `tests/screenshot-image.sh` so this framebuffer check becomes a committed, repeatable test rather than a scratch script in `/tmp`.
