[2026-10-13T17:22:20.487Z] [ASSISTANT] I found it, and it's not a theme misconfiguration. Here's the diagnosis.

## Why the Fedora logo appears

Plymouth is configured correctly — `/etc/plymouth/plymouthd.conf` says `Theme=openqos`, and even the copy baked into your initramfs says `Theme=openqos`. The problem is that **the OpenQOS theme isn't in the initramfs**:

| Check | Result |
| --- | --- |
| `/etc/plymouth/plymouthd.conf` in the image | `Theme=openqos` |
| `plymouthd.conf` inside the initramfs | `Theme=openqos` |
| Themes inside the initramfs | `bgrt`, `details`, `spinner`, `text` — **no `openqos`** |
| `/usr/share/pixmaps/system-logo-white.png` in the initramfs | **present** (9523 bytes, from `fedora-logos`) |

Your boot has two stages. The first several seconds run Plymouth from the **initramfs**; after the switch to the real root it re-reads `/etc/plymouth/plymouthd.conf`. In the initramfs it is told to load a theme that isn't there, so it falls back to the themes that *are* bundled — and `spinner`'s watermark is the Fedora logo.

On top of that, dracut's Plymouth script installs the Fedora logo file into the initramfs **unconditionally**:

```sh
PLYMOUTH_LOGO_FILE="/usr/share/pixmaps/system-logo-white.png"
PLYMOUTH_THEME=$(plymouth-set-default-theme)
test -e "${PLYMOUTH_LOGO_FILE}" && inst_simple "${PLYMOUTH_LOGO_FILE}"
```

Note the asymmetry: the *theme* is only copied under conditions, but the *Fedora logo* is always copied. So even the fallback path is hard-coded to Fedora artwork.

This is not architecture-specific — the aarch64 build has the same defect.

## What else is Fedora-branded

Beyond the splash, the image currently carries:

- **`fedora-logos`** — `/usr/share/pixmaps/fedora-logo*`, `fedora_whitelogo*`, `fedora-logo-sprite*`, `system-logo-white.png`, and `fedora-logo-icon.png` across the hicolor icon themes.
- **`plymouth-theme-spinner`** — the Fedora-logo watermark you're seeing.
- **`/etc/fedora-release` and `/etc/system-release`** — both read "Fedora release 44 (Forty Four)".
- **`/usr/share/backgrounds/f44`** — Fedora's wallpaper set.
- **`console-login-helper-messages-issuegen`** — this is what injected the vsock line into your login banner.
- An Anaconda installer icon.

Already correct: `os-release` (`ID=openqos`, `LOGO=openqos-logo`), the GRUB menu (`Start OpenQOS`, `--class openqos`), and the Plasma look-and-feel.

## Concerns I want to flag before touching anything

1. **`fedora-logos` cannot simply be deleted.** Repos show it's required by `gdm`, `calamares`, `breeze-icon-theme-fedora`, `gnome-shell-extension-background-logo`, `cinnamon`, `deepin-desktop-base` and more. The fix has to be `Obsoletes: fedora-logos` + `Provides: fedora-logos` on `openqos-branding`, or shipping our files at those exact paths — and RPM refuses two packages owning one path without an Obsoletes.

2. **`fedora-release` cannot be deleted either** — `fedora-repos` and `setup` require it. So the "Fedora release 44" text has to be taken over by `openqos-release`, which means giving it an `Obsoletes`. Note its spec *deliberately* has none and documents why; that reasoning was about the 61 identity flavours, not about `fedora-release` itself, so this needs a separate decision rather than copying.

3. **The project already records this as unverified.** `branding/plymouth/openqos/openqos.plymouth` says: *"this VM has no DRM device, so Plymouth falls back to its text renderer and never draws any of this."* The theme was never seen rendering — which is consistent with nobody noticing it never reaches the initramfs.

4. **Licensing.** Fedora's logos are `LicenseRef-Fedora-Logos`, not freely redistributable. Removing them is the right direction, but `docs/licensing.md` should be followed.

5. **Scope.** Specs + description + initramfs generation + rebuild + visual verification. That's milestone-sized, not a single patch.

