[2026-10-13T18:34:01.499Z] [TOOL_CALL] {"id":"call_00_O75g1AUVNEMUdhYsStQF1723","name":"write_file","args":{"content":"# OpenQOS: put the OpenQOS boot splash theme, and what it needs to draw, into the\n# initramfs.\n#\n# Why this file exists\n# --------------------\n# The theme is selected through KIWI's <bootsplash-theme>, which runs\n# `plymouth-set-default-theme openqos` inside the image. That is why\n# /etc/plymouth/plymouthd.conf reads Theme=openqos, and it is enough for Plymouth\n# once the real root filesystem is mounted.\n#\n# It is not enough for the first seconds of the boot. The splash on screen before\n# the switch to root is drawn by Plymouth running *out of the initramfs*, and that\n# copy reads its theme from the initramfs rather than from /etc. Fedora's dracut\n# tries to copy the configured theme in, but only under conditions, while it copies\n# /usr/share/pixmaps/system-logo-white.png from fedora-logos unconditionally.\n#\n# The result was an initramfs that set Theme=openqos and contained no openqos\n# theme, so Plymouth fell back to a theme that was bundled -- and the spinner\n# watermark is the Fedora logo. That was the Fedora logo on the boot screen: not a\n# misconfigured theme, but a theme that never arrived.\n#\n# install_items is dracut's documented way to name files that must be present\n# regardless of dracut's own heuristics, so none of this depends on them any more.\n#\n# Why the plugin and the renderers are listed too\n# ----------------------------------------------\n# The theme files alone are not enough, and the reason is worth recording because\n# it is not obvious from the initramfs:\n#\n#   * Most of this list used to arrive *by accident*. Fedora's\n#     plymouth-theme-spinner requires plymouth-plugin-two-step at an exact\n#     version, and dracut installed the plugin and the libraries it needs as part\n#     of handling that theme. OpenQOS excludes plymouth-theme-spinner so the\n#     Fedora logo stops appearing at boot, and doing so silently removed\n#     two-step.so as well. two-step is the module our own theme declares, so the\n#     theme would have been present and still unloadable.\n#\n#   * The renderers are what actually paint the screen, and dracut does not\n#     install them for a theme it did not choose. Without them Plymouth has\n#     nothing to draw with in the initramfs and falls back to text.\n#\n# Paths are /usr/lib64, which is where Fedora puts these on both of the\n# architectures this distribution builds for, x86_64 and aarch64. dracut warns\n# about an install_items entry that does not exist, so a profile that installs\n# openqos-branding without Plymouth will produce warnings rather than a failure.\n\ninstall_items+=\" \\\n    /usr/share/plymouth/themes/openqos/openqos.plymouth \\\n    /usr/share/plymouth/themes/openqos/watermark.png \\\n    /usr/lib64/plymouth/two-step.so \\\n    /usr/lib64/plymouth/label-freetype.so \\\n    /usr/lib64/plymouth/renderers/drm.so \\\n    /usr/lib64/plymouth/renderers/frame-buffer.so \\\n    /usr/lib64/libply-splash-graphics.so.5 \\\n\"\n","path":"branding/dracut/50-openqos-plymouth.conf"}}
