[2026-10-13T19:20:23.989Z] [TOOL_RESULT] {"id":"call_00_c40kep34ad43rQe5XLSj5263","name":"read_file","elapsed_ms":1,"ok":true,"output":"598:   image package is considered.\n599: \n600: Obsoleting a single flavour is the intuitive approach and does not work: it removes\n601: one candidate out of 61, so dnf picks another and the conflict reappears under a\n602: different package name. Repo priority does not decide it either, since our\n603: repository and Fedora's all share one priority.\n604: \n605: ## 6. Reproducibility and verification\n606: \n607: * Pin the exact Fedora release and compose; record it in the build manifest.\n608: * Record SHA-256 digests of every downloaded upstream artefact and **verify against\n609:   Fedora's PGP-signed `*-CHECKSUM` files** before use.\n610: * Build scripts must be idempotent and must fail loudly rather than silently\n611:   producing a different image.\n612: * Test images are verified by booting them, not by trusting that a build command\n613:   exited 0. See §7.\n614: \n615: ### 6.1 Password hashing\n616: \n617: KIWI's `<user password=\"...\" pwdformat=\"plain\">` hashes with the C library's default\n618: crypt method, which on current glibc is **MD5**: the image shipped a `$1$` hash in\n619: `/etc/shadow`. KIWI offers no stronger option — `pwdformat` accepts only `plain` and\n620: `encrypted`, and `encrypted` means committing a hash, which is no better than\n621: committing the password.\n622: \n623: The description therefore sets no usable password: `password=\"!\"` with\n624: `pwdformat=\"encrypted\"`, which KIWI passes through verbatim (`useradd -p '!'`) and\n625: which is the standard marker for an account that cannot be logged into. The attribute\n626: cannot be omitted — KIWI's schema makes it required.\n627: \n628: Credentials are set in [`kiwi/config.sh`](../kiwi/config.sh), where `chpasswd` can be\n629: told the algorithm: `--crypt-method YESCRYPT`, named explicitly rather than left to a\n630: default, so this cannot silently become something weaker if shadow-utils changes what\n631: it picks. The result is a `$y$` hash.\n632: \n633: Two assertions run in that same script, so a mistake fails "}
