[2026-10-13T20:17:24.449Z] [ASSISTANT] Building. While that runs, let me deal with the one thing I can't deliver, because it's a hard constraint rather than a setback.

## aarch64 cannot be built on this machine

Three independent confirmations:

1. **`scripts/build-image.sh` says so.** Lines 79–90: *"KIWI builds an image for the architecture of the machine it runs on — there is no cross-architecture option"*, and it hard-fails on any host arch outside `aarch64|x86_64`.

2. **This host is x86_64.** `uname -m` → `x86_64`.

3. **Emulation isn't available either.** No `qemu-user-static` (only `qemu-system-x86_64`), no aarch64 binfmt handler registered, and:

```
$ docker run --rm --platform linux/arm64 fedora:44 uname -m
exec /usr/sbin/uname: exec format error
```

The project already documents the correct answer, in `tools/lima/openqos-dev.yaml`:

> An aarch64 Fedora 44 virtual machine used to BUILD OpenQOS images.
> … aarch64 media can only be produced on an aarch64 host … the Mac is aarch64 and so is this Mac, so this is hardware acceleration, not emulation.

So the intended path is the **Apple Silicon Mac + Lima VM**, which is exactly what I'm not on. `limactl` isn't installed here either.

Your options:

- **Build aarch64 on the aarch64 host** (the documented path). Everything I changed is architecture-neutral — the `fedora-release*` glob, the `system-release(44)` capability, the `system-backgrounds-kde` capability, the dracut `install_items` (both supported arches use `/usr/lib64`) — so `scripts/build-image.sh --profile OpenQOS-KDE-Live --type iso` on that machine will pick up all of it unchanged, and now also write to `dist/` with `aarch64` in the filename.
- **Install emulation here and try anyway.** `docker run --privileged --rm tonistiigi/binfmt --install arm64`, then build inside an arm64 container. This modifies the host (privileged, registers binfmt handlers) and the build would run entirely under QEMU user emulation — a full package transaction plus EROFS compression of a 3.5 GB image. I'd expect hours, with a real chance that KIWI's image-type tooling misbehaves under emulation. I'd want your go-ahead before touching the host like that.

Tell me which and I'll proceed. In the meantime the x86_64 ISO is building into `dist/`.
