The `Fedora` symlink is gone, which is the `%posttrans` scriptlet doing its job after the transaction completes — so it can't be undone by `kde-settings-plasma` installing later. As agreed, `rpm -V kde-settings-plasma` will report that one file missing for as long as `openqos-branding` is installed; the doc records it.

## The guard swap is the part worth knowing about

`scripts/build-packages.sh` had a guard that verified `openqos-release` provided `fedora-release-identity = 44-18`. Removing that Provide would have left a guard checking for a capability nobody wanted. Rather than delete it, I retargeted it to the requirement that now does the work:

```
Release guard: system-release and system-release(44) satisfied by openqos-release-44-18.fc44.noarch.rpm
```

So the "fail loudly instead of surfacing as a mysterious image-build error" property is preserved — it now guards the capability that `fedora-repos` actually asks for, and which moves with each Fedora release.

## aarch64 — blocked, and here's exactly why

Not a setback I can engineer around. Three independent confirmations:

1. `scripts/build-image.sh:79-90` — *"KIWI builds an image for the architecture of the machine it runs on — there is no cross-architecture option"*, and it exits non-zero for any host arch that isn't `aarch64|x86_64`.
2. This host is `x86_64`.
3. Emulation is unavailable: no `qemu-user-static`, no aarch64 binfmt handler, and `docker run --platform linux/arm64 fedora:44 uname -m` → `exec format error`.

Your own `tools/lima/openqos-dev.yaml` states the intended path: an aarch64 Fedora 44 Lima VM, hardware-accelerated *because the Mac is aarch64*. `limactl` isn't installed here.

**Everything I changed is arch-neutral** — the `fedora-release*`/`generic-release*` globs, the `system-release(44)` capability, `system-backgrounds-kde`, and the dracut `install_items` (both supported arches use `/usr/lib64`). So running the same command on the aarch64 host will pick all of it up unchanged, and now writes to `dist/` with `aarch64` in the filename.

If you'd rather I force it here: `docker run --privileged --rm tonistiigi/binfmt --install arm64` then build in an arm64 container. That modifies your host (privileged, registers binfmt handlers) and the build would run wholly under QEMU emulation — a full package transaction plus EROFS compression of a 3.5 GB image. I'd expect hours and a real chance of failure in KIWI's image tooling. Say the word and I'l
