Let me write a good answer:

**Why the first boot is slow**

Several things compound, and only one of them is a one-off:

1. The live root is a compressed filesystem (the ISOs use erofs inside `LiveOS/squashfs.img`). Every file read is decompressed on the fly, on the guest CPU. That's the steady-state cost and it doesn't go away on later boots.
2. First
[/THINK]
[2026-10-13T21:16:12.430Z] [THINK]
 boot specifically runs one-time work that later boots don't: systemd generating machine-id and first-boot units, KDE building its service cache (`kbuildsycoca6`), fontconfig building its cache, desktop/icon database updates, and NetworkManager waiting on DHCP. That's typically the bulk of the "first
[/THINK]
[2026-10-13T21:16:12.841Z] [THINK]
 boot is slower" effect.
3. If booting from the ISO file in VirtualBox, the very first read is cold — the host has to read 3.3 GB from disk. The second boot is much faster because the host page cache holds the ISO if you have the RAM. That alone often explains "first boot slow, second fine".

**Is it normal?**
[/THINK]
[2026-10-13T21:16:13.242Z] [THINK]
 Yes — for a live KDE ISO of this size in VirtualBox, a first boot of a couple of minutes is normal. It's also worth checking the VM has enough RAM (KDE + guest wants ≥ 
[/THINK]
[2026-10-13T21:16:13.654Z] [THINK]
4 GB) and more than one vCPU, since decompression is CPU-bound.

